= BITwiki Constitution =

The Constitution records the constraints that should survive changes in interface, model, extension stack, taxonomy, workflow, and runtime.

The [[BITwiki:Charter|Charter]] states how BITwiki intends to conduct knowledge work. This Constitution states what the system must protect while doing so.

== Preamble ==

BITwiki is a living knowledge field: information is gathered, structured, related, tested, corrected, preserved, and reused through human and machine cognition.

The V1 Constitution described BITwiki as an '''intelligence factory''', a '''field for nullification of non-signals''', and a '''concentrator of signals'''. V2 preserves the durable meaning of those formulations:

* useful intelligence should be externalized into durable structures;
* noise should not be mistaken for knowledge merely because it is abundant;
* compression must preserve meaning;
* the system should make knowledge easier to inspect, query, relate, and improve.

No implementation detail receives constitutional status merely because it existed in one deployment.

== Article I — Purpose and scope ==

BITwiki exists to externalize useful intelligence into '''queryable, navigable, machine-readable, human-legible structures while preserving depth, nuance, provenance, and epistemic humility'''.

It functions as:

* durable semantic memory;
* a knowledge graph and reference surface;
* a bridge between human and machine cognition;
* a testbed for recursive knowledge architectures;
* an environment where theory, evidence, history, implementation, and criticism can remain connected.

The system may span scientific, technical, philosophical, historical, cultural, and practical domains. Scope is determined by durable knowledge value and appropriate sourcing, not by a permanently closed subject list.

== Article II — Knowledge remains revisable ==

'''No claim is final.''' Pages, ontologies, taxonomies, models, workflows, constitutional interpretations, and agent outputs remain corrigible.

Revision is not an embarrassment to conceal. Revision history is part of the memory of the system.

A page can become more precise without pretending its previous state never existed. A framework can be superseded while remaining historically important. A source can be corrected without erasing why it once mattered.

== Article III — Evidence and provenance are first-class ==

The system must preserve the difference between a claim and its support.

Where the significance of a claim requires it, a reader or agent should be able to inspect:

* source or origin;
* evidence and method;
* perspective or assumptions;
* uncertainty and limitations;
* relevant contradictions;
* validation or review history;
* material changes over time.

V1 expressed this through provenance requirements and later through the concept of '''confidence trails'''. V2 treats that underlying traversability as more important than any one scoring convention.

== Article IV — Ontology, epistemology, and axiology remain distinct ==

BITwiki must not collapse different kinds of questions into one metadata system.

* '''Ontology''' concerns what kinds of things and relations the system recognizes.
* '''Epistemology''' concerns how claims are acquired, justified, interpreted, qualified, and revised.
* '''Axiology''' concerns what is valued, prioritized, protected, optimized, or pursued.

Semantics, logic, language, interpretation, workflow, and implementation connect these axes but do not make them identical.

See [[BITwiki:Foundational triad]].

== Article V — Ontology is open, explicit, and relational ==

V1 experimented with canonical lists of entity types such as Topic, Idea, Implementation, Project, Protocol, Dataset, Person, Organization, Event, Publication, and Tool.

Those lists are valuable design evidence, but the Constitution does not freeze one list forever.

The durable requirements are:

# entity types must carry a clear semantic purpose;
# relationships should be explicit when they materially improve retrieval or reasoning;
# ontology should be extendable without destroying compatibility unnecessarily;
# a schema term should not exist merely because a document once proposed it;
# implementation must distinguish a documented design from an actually instantiated MediaWiki/SMW object.

The exhaustive V1 audit demonstrates why this distinction matters: V1 documented a much richer semantic system than it physically instantiated in the Property, Concept, Module, and Template namespaces.

== Article VI — Epistemic foundations ==

### Radical skepticism

Claims should be testable or challengeable in ways appropriate to their domain. A useful claim should make clear what evidence, argument, or observation bears on it and, where possible, what would weaken or overturn it.

### Radical realism / operational adequacy

Consensus and credential are not substitutes for reality-testing. Evidence, logical coherence, predictive success, explanatory power, survival of refutation, and operational validity may all matter depending on the question.

No single one of those is elevated into a universal test for every form of knowledge.

### Epistemic humility

The map is not the territory. Models have limits. Confidence should not outrun evidence.

Axiomatic or foundational status means that a proposition is currently serving as a system premise—not that it has become metaphysically immune to revision.

### Explicit uncertainty

Hypothesis, emerging evidence, established result, verified result, assumption, interpretation, metaphor, and contested claim should not be rendered indistinguishable when the difference matters.

The canonical implementation belongs in [[BITwiki:Epistemics]].

== Article VII — Recursive architecture must be purposeful ==

V1 described BITwiki through several recursive design ideas, including '''toroidal validation''' and '''semantic fractalization'''. These are preserved as conceptual architecture rather than mandatory metaphysical facts.

Their durable insight is that a knowledge system can validate and improve itself across scales:

* claims can be checked against other claims and evidence;
* page structures can expose reusable patterns;
* domain models can be compared across contexts;
* the system can inspect its own ontology, epistemics, workflow, and failure modes.

Recursion must serve understanding. Infinite self-reference without useful refinement is not an objective.

== Article VIII — Human agency is an invariant ==

Human–AI systems should increase human clarity, dignity, self-respect, and agency rather than manufacture dependence, compliance, coercion, or hidden loss of responsibility.

Agents may research, synthesize, transform, validate, organize, and maintain knowledge. Their output remains subject to provenance, review, correction, and scope.

Automation is infrastructure for cognition, not a constitutional excuse to obscure who decided what should become canon.

== Article IX — Perspective may be plural; deception is not ==

BITwiki may preserve competing models, schools, frameworks, worldviews, interpretations, and speculative structures.

Pluralism does not permit:

* presenting metaphor as measurement;
* presenting speculation as established fact;
* suppressing material contradictory evidence;
* hiding perspective behind false claims of neutrality;
* manufacturing certainty for rhetorical effect.

Multiple perspectives should improve resolution, not eliminate standards.

== Article X — Knowledge must remain transposable, modular, and composable ==

The canonical parent page should remain coherent while meaningful portions can be reused independently when that serves a real purpose.

Book Matter, transclusion, semantic properties, templates, categories, portals, and other mechanisms should support:

* progressive disclosure;
* selective retrieval;
* reuse without context loss;
* machine queryability;
* human comprehension.

'''Modularity does not mean fragmentation.'''

== Article XI — Implementation serves the knowledge model ==

MediaWiki namespaces, categories, templates, Semantic MediaWiki properties, concepts, modules, extensions, portals, scripts, and interfaces are mechanisms. They are not themselves the ontology.

A constitutional distinction must not disappear simply because an extension changes. Conversely, an obsolete implementation pattern does not become permanent merely because it appeared in an earlier constitutional draft.

V1 provides an instructive example: constitutional and schema documents named properties such as <code>Has entity type</code>, <code>Has epistemic status</code>, <code>Has confidence score</code>, <code>Has source</code>, and <code>Has related</code>, while the exhaustive implementation audit shows that the authored Property namespace was far smaller. V2 therefore preserves the intended semantic dimensions without falsely claiming those historical objects were fully deployed.

== Article XII — Governance and arbitration must be explicit ==

V1 repeatedly stated that BITCORE maintained editorial authority and rejected the fiction of distributed autonomy. V2 preserves the durable principle rather than hard-coding every historical role:

'''the system must state where canonical authority resides.'''

Stewardship and dispute resolution should operate through evidence, transparent rationale, and bounded authority. No governance mechanism should be inferred merely from the presence of agents, a forum, a blockchain, or a public repository.

When escalation is necessary, there must be a terminating authority rather than infinite procedural regress.

== Article XIII — Principles in tension ==

The V1 Constitution explicitly recognized that good principles can conflict. V2 preserves this practice.

=== Transparency vs. quality ===
Prefer inspectable imperfection over opaque perfection. Low-quality transparent work can be corrected; opaque claims cannot be meaningfully audited.

=== Inclusion vs. rigor ===
Preserve legitimate perspectives, but do not lower evidentiary standards merely to make every view appear equally supported.

=== Stability vs. evolution ===
Prefer measured evolution. Stagnation ossifies; uncontrolled change destroys continuity.

=== Compression vs. nuance ===
Remove non-signal, but do not compress away distinctions that carry meaning.

=== Automation vs. agency ===
Use automation to increase capability without silently transferring responsibility or purpose away from human stewardship.

== Article XIV — Public and protected knowledge remain bounded ==

Publication is an explicit boundary transition.

Public repositories and public wiki pages must not silently absorb private memory, credentials, access-control internals, unpublished sensitive material, identity-bound state, or other information that has not been deliberately cleared for publication.

Source fidelity never overrides legitimate privacy and security boundaries.

== Article XV — Amendment, versioning, and falsification ==

The Constitution applies to itself.

Major constitutional change must preserve:

* prior versions;
* diff or revision history;
* rationale for change;
* relevant source lineage;
* distinction between philosophical change and technical migration.

The Constitution should be reconsidered if its epistemic markers become performative rather than substantive, if governance becomes opaque, if quality rules are applied selectively, if source lineage is routinely lost, or if the system becomes unable to correct itself.

== Historical constitutional architecture ==

The V1 consolidated Constitution included specific structures—six canonical entity types, five-tier epistemic stratification, toroidal validation, semantic fractalization, specific SMW properties, steward arbitration, and explicit BITCORE authority. These are not erased.

They are preserved as historical constitutional design and remain available in the exact archived source and public revision history. V2 promotes only those elements that survive comparison with the actual implementation and current knowledge architecture.

